-- title:
--   Integrality of a generic element whose finite-part image is integral
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative domain with fraction field $K$ (i.e. $K$ is a field with an $R$-algebra structure making it a fraction ring of $R$), and let $H$ be a commutative $R$-algebra. Let $e, f \in H$ be idempotent elements with $f e = e$, and assume that every nonzero $d \in R$ acts surjectively on the ideal $H(1-e)$ in the sense that for each $d \neq 0$ and each $y \in H$ there exists $z \in H$ with $d \cdot (z(1-e)) = y(1-e)$. Let $S$ be a commutative $R$-algebra and $\pi_f : H \to S$ a surjective $R$-algebra homomorphism whose kernel is the principal ideal generated by $1 - e$. Finally, let $\beta \in K \otimes_R H$ satisfy $\beta \cdot (1 \otimes f) = \beta$, and suppose there is $a \in S$ with $(\mathrm{id}_K \otimes \pi_f)(\beta) = 1 \otimes a$ in $K \otimes_R S$. The conclusion is that $\beta$ is integral and compatibly so: there exists $h \in H$ with $\beta = 1 \otimes h$ and $\pi_f(h) = a$.
--
--   An elementary commutative-algebra statement about an idempotent $e$ cutting out a "finite part" $H \to S \cong He$ of an $R$-algebra, with the complementary part $H(1-e)$ divisible by the nonzero elements of $R$: a generic element of $K \otimes_R H$ whose image on the finite part is integral is itself integral. It is used in the proof that the relevant kernel is preserved under the maps attached to a finite-part idempotent, in [`HopfAlgebra.map_hopfKer_eq_hopfKer_of_finitePartIdempotent`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.map_hopfKer_eq_hopfKer_of_finitePartIdempotent).
